    13	## 1. Population, target and statement
    14	
    15	Throughout, n is the bit length, X = 2^n and P = 2^{n/2}.
    16	
    17	**Population.**
    18	
    19	  S_n = {(p, q) : p < q ≤ 2p primes, 2^{n−1} ≤ pq < 2^n}.
    20	
    21	It is counted by pairs, equivalently by semiprimes N = pq. Then p ∈ (P/2, P) and
    22	q ∈ (P/√2, √2 P). Counting with the PNT in [0.85X′, 0.9X′] × [1.2X′, 1.25X′],
    23	X′ = 2^{(n−1)/2}, gives |S_n| ≥ c₀ P²/n² with an absolute c₀ > 0 for n ≥ n₀.
    24	
    25	**Target.** A deterministic algorithm 𝒜_{n,ε} with the following properties.
    26	- It receives N (n bits) and outputs either a nontrivial divisor of N or FAIL.
    27	- Its worst-case cost on every n-bit input is N^{1/6+O(ε)} bit operations.
    28	- It outputs FAIL on at most O(N^{−ε})·|S_n| pairs of S_n.
    29	
    30	**Theorem D′ (proved; independent Opus critic review in §7).** Fix ε ∈ (0, 1/60]. There
    31	are n₀(ε) and absolute constants C, c such that for n ≥ n₀ the algorithm DCL-det_{n,ε} of
    32	§2 has the following properties.
    33	- (i) **Correctness.** On every input it outputs a nontrivial divisor of N or FAIL.
    34	- (ii) **Cost.** On every n-bit input it uses O(n^c·2^{(1/6+5ε/6)n}) bit operations, i.e.
    35	  N^{1/6+5ε/6}(log N)^{O(1)}.
    36	- (iii) **Success.** It outputs FAIL on at most C·2^{−εn}·|S_n| pairs (p,q) ∈ S_n.
    37	
    38	Equivalently: for every δ ∈ (0, 1/72], cost N^{1/6+δ+o(1)} with an exceptional fraction
    39	O(N^{−6δ/5}).
    40	
    41	**Corollary D″ (proved, same proof; polylog regime).** For every fixed a > 0, the same
    42	algorithm with H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉ (and T = 2^{n/4}n^{−1−a} in the analysis) has cost
    43	N^{1/6}(log N)^{O(1+a)} and fails on O((log N)^{−2a}) of S_n.
