    53	## 2. The algorithm DCL-det_{n,ε} (operational; `src/dcltheorem/algorithm.py`)
    54	
    55	### 2.1 Parameters
    56	
    57	All parameters are functions of n and ε only; nothing depends on N.
    58	
    59	- H = ⌈n·2^{εn/2}⌉.
    60	- V = {(A,B) ∈ [1,H]² : gcd(A,B) = 1, A ≠ B}. Then H²/2 ≤ |V| ≤ H² for large H.
    61	- Y = ⌈2^{n/6}H^{−1/3}⌉.
    62	- For k = 0, 1, 2, 3: 𝒫_k = {ℓ prime : ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4), 2^kY < ℓ ≤ 2^{k+1}Y} and
    63	  L_k = ∏_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} ℓ².
    64	- m̄_k = ⌊span̄/(2^kY)²⌋ + 2, with span̄ = ⌊(5/2)·H·(2^{n/2}+1)⌋ + 2H + 3. This is an upper
    65	  bound for every window width in block k (§2.2).
    66	- The base is β = 2.
    67	
    68	### 2.2 Steps
    69	
    70	Input N (n bits). If N is even, output 2. Set a₀ = ⌈√(N/2)⌉ and b₀ = ⌊√N⌋. Every (p,q) ∈ S_n
    71	has p ∈ [a₀, b₀].
    72	
    73	For v = (A,B) ∈ V put:
    74	- Lo_v = ⌊2√(ABN)⌋. By AM–GM, Lo_v ≤ F_v(x) := Bx + AN/x for all x > 0.
    75	- Hi_v = max_{x∈{a₀,b₀}} ⌈F_v(x)⌉. By convexity, F_v ≤ Hi_v on [a₀, b₀].
    76	- Then Hi_v − Lo_v ≤ (B + √2A)√N + 2H + 2 ≤ span̄.
    77	
    78	For each block k = 0, 1, 2, 3:
    79	
    80	1. **Exponent tree.**
    81	   - Build the balanced integer product tree of {ℓ² : ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k}.
    82	   - Push 2 down the tree: a node holding 2^{L/∏node} hands 2^{L/∏node·∏sibling} to each
    83	     child. This yields u_ℓ = 2^{L_k/ℓ²} mod N for all ℓ.
    84	   - Set γ = u_{ℓ₀}^{ℓ₀²} = 2^{L_k} mod N.
    85	   - If ℓ | N for some ℓ, output ℓ.
    86	2. **Targets.** For ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k and v ∈ V:
    87	   - a := A N B^{−1} mod ℓ. If a^{(ℓ−1)/2} ≢ 1 (mod ℓ), skip.
    88	   - Otherwise r₀ = a^{(ℓ+1)/4} mod ℓ, and the roots of Br² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) are {r₀, ℓ−r₀}.
    89	   - For each root r:
    90	     - s = N r^{−1} mod ℓ² and c = (Br + As) mod ℓ²;
    91	     - g_lo = ⌈(Lo_v − c)/ℓ²⌉ ≥ 0 and g_hi = ⌊(Hi_v − c)/ℓ²⌋;
    92	     - W_τ = g_hi − g_lo + 1 ≤ span̄/ℓ² + 1 ≤ m̄_k.
    93	   - The target is τ = (v, ℓ, r). Write T_k for the number of targets: T_k ≤ 2|V||𝒫_k|.
    94	3. **Baby length.** Choose m ∈ [1, m̄_k] as a function of N. The implementation minimises
    95	   m + Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m⌉ over a grid ending at max_τ W_τ. The proof needs only m ≤ m̄_k.
    96	4. **Giants.**
    97	   - Precompute w_ℓ = u_ℓ^N for each ℓ.
    98	   - For each target and 0 ≤ j < ⌈W_τ/m⌉:
    99	
   100	       G_{τ,j} = w_ℓ^A · u_ℓ^{B−c+ℓ²} · γ^{−(g_lo+1)} · γ^{−jm}  ≡  u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}·γ^{−(g_lo+jm)}  (mod N).
   101	
   102	     The congruence holds because u_ℓ^{ℓ²} = γ. γ^{−1} exists because gcd(2,N) = 1.
   103	5. **Collision detection.**
   104	   - f(X) = ∏_{0≤i<m}(X − γ^i) ∈ (Z/N)[X], built by a product tree.
   105	   - f(G_{τ,j}) for all giants, by a remainder tree. Only monic divisors are used, so this
   106	     is valid over Z/N.
   107	   - For each giant, d = gcd(f(G), N). If 1 < d < N, output d.
   108	
   109	Output FAIL if no block yields a divisor.
   110	
   111	**Remarks.**
   112	- (a) No order is computed, assumed or certified. No randomness is used.
   113	- (b) The symmetric weight (1,1) is excluded. For A = B the true giant always collides mod
   114	  q as well (Lemma 4 with A − B = 0), so it can never be one-sided.
   115	- (c) The four bands are needed only for Lemma 6, which guarantees a "clean" band.
   116	
   117	## 3. Lemmas
   118	
   119	Fix (p,q) ∈ S_n, N = pq, a block k, and write L = L_k, u = u_ℓ and d := ord_q(2).
   120	
   121	**Lemma 1 (class lock; HH 2022 Prop. 3.3).** Let ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k, (A,B) ∈ V and r a root, and
   122	suppose p ≡ r (mod ℓ). Then g := (Bp + Aq − c)/ℓ² ∈ Z.
   123	
   124	*Proof.*
   125	- ℓ > Y > H ≥ A, B, and ℓ < p, so ℓ ∤ ABN. Write p = r + ℓx.
   126	- Mod ℓ²: p^{−1} ≡ r^{−1}(1 − ℓxr^{−1}), so q = Np^{−1} ≡ s − ℓx·s r^{−1}.
   127	- Hence Bp + Aq ≡ c + ℓx(B − A s r^{−1}).
   128	- s r^{−1} ≡ N r^{−2} (mod ℓ) and Br² ≡ AN, so B − ANr^{−2} ≡ 0 (mod ℓ). The last term is
   129	  therefore ≡ 0 (mod ℓ²). ∎
   130	
   131	**Coverage criterion.** p mod ℓ is one of the computed roots ⇔ Bp² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) ⇔
   132	ℓ | Bp − Aq (cancel p, since ℓ ∤ p). We say τ = (v, ℓ, p mod ℓ) *covers* p.
   133	
   134	**Lemma 2 (window).** If τ covers p, then g_lo ≤ g ≤ g_hi. Put i_full = g − g_lo,
   135	j* = ⌊i_full/m⌋ and i* = i_full − j*m ∈ [0, m).
   136	
   137	*Proof.* p ∈ [a₀, b₀], so Lo_v ≤ F_v(p) = Bp + Aq ≤ Hi_v. ∎
   138	
   139	**Lemma 3 (mod p).** If τ covers p, then G_{τ,j*} ≡ γ^{i*} (mod p). No hypothesis on any
   140	order is needed.
   141	
   142	*Proof.*
   143	- B + AN − (Bp + Aq) = (p − 1)(Aq − B), and 2^{p−1} ≡ 1 (mod p).
   144	- So u^{B+AN−c} ≡ 2^{(L/ℓ²)(Bp+Aq−c)} = 2^{Lg} = γ^g (mod p).
   145	- Multiply by γ^{−(g_lo + j*m)}. ∎
   146	
   147	**Lemma 4 (mod q).** If τ covers p, then G_{τ,j*} ≡ γ^{i*}·u^{(A−B)(p−1)} (mod q).
   148	
   149	*Proof.*
   150	- Mod q − 1: N ≡ p, so B + AN − c ≡ B + Ap − c.
   151	- Also ℓ²g = Bp + Aq − c ≡ Bp + A − c.
   152	- The difference is (A − B)(p − 1). Multiplying by L/ℓ² preserves the congruence mod q − 1,
   153	  and 2^{q−1} ≡ 1 (mod q). ∎
   154	
   155	**Lemma 5 (one-sided extraction).** Call the true giant G = G_{τ,j*} *FULL* if
   156	G ≡ γ^{i′} (mod q) for some i′ ∈ [0, m). If τ covers p and G is not FULL, then
   157	gcd(f(G), N) = p.
   158	
   159	*Proof.*
   160	- f(G) = ∏_i (G − γ^i).
   161	- The factor i = i* vanishes mod p (Lemma 3).
   162	- Mod q, no factor vanishes, by definition, and Z/q is a field.
   163	- So p | f(G) and q ∤ f(G). ∎
   164	
   165	**Lemma 6 (clean band).** Call block k *clean for q* if no ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k divides q − 1. If
   166	Y⁴ > √2P, at least one of the four blocks is clean for q.
   167	
   168	*Proof.* Every band prime exceeds Y. Four distinct band primes dividing q − 1 would give
   169	q − 1 > Y⁴ > √2P > q. So at most 3 blocks contain a prime factor of q − 1. ∎
   170	
   171	Asymptotically: Y⁴ ≥ 2^{2n/3}H^{−4/3}, and 2^{n/6} > √2 H^{4/3} = √2 n^{4/3}2^{2εn/3}
   172	for ε < 1/4 and n large.
   173	
   174	**Lemma 7 (FULL in a clean block).** If block k is clean for q, then the true giant is FULL
   175	iff d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ for some δ ∈ [−i*, m−1−i*] ⊂ (−m, m).
   176	
   177	*Proof.*
   178	- By Lemma 4, FULL ⇔ u^{(A−B)(p−1)} ≡ γ^{δ} (mod q) with δ = i′ − i*.
   179	- Equivalently 2^{(L/ℓ²)[(A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ]} ≡ 1 (mod q).
   180	- Since d | q − 1 and gcd(L, q − 1) = 1, gcd(d, L/ℓ²) = 1. ∎
   181	
   182	**Lemma 8 (small orders are rare; elementary).** For Q₀ ≥ 2 and T ≥ 1,
   183	
   184	  #{q > Q₀ prime : ord_q(2) ≤ T} ≤ T(T+1)/(2 log₂ Q₀).
   185	
   186	*Proof.* Such a q divides 2^k − 1 for some k ≤ T. Since 2^k − 1 < 2^k, it has fewer than
   187	k/log₂Q₀ prime factors > Q₀. Sum over k ≤ T. ∎
   188	
   189	**Lemma 9 (coverage: the arithmetic large sieve, in the audit's repaired form).** Fix q and
   190	k. Let U_k(q) be the set of integers p ∈ [P/2, P] that no target of block k covers, i.e.
   191	ℓ ∤ Bp − Aq for all (v, ℓ) ∈ V × 𝒫_k. For n ≥ n₀,
   192	
   193	  |U_k(q)| ≤ (P/2 + 1 + 4^{k+1}Y²)/J_k,  J_k ≥ |V|·Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ ≥ c₁H²/n.
   194	
   195	*Proof.*
   196	- For ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k, ℓ ∤ q, so ℓ | Bp − Aq ⇔ p ≡ AqB^{−1} (mod ℓ).
   197	- These |V| classes are distinct: AqB^{−1} ≡ A′qB′^{−1} implies ℓ | AB′ − A′B, and
   198	  0 < |AB′ − A′B| < H² < Y < ℓ. This uses Y > H², i.e. 2^{n/6} > n^{7/3}2^{7εn/6}, true for
   199	  ε < 1/7 and n large.
   200	- So U_k(q) avoids ω(ℓ) = |V| < ℓ classes mod every ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k.
   201	- The arithmetic large sieve (Montgomery 1968; Montgomery–Vaughan 1973, constant
   202	  N₀ − 1 + Q² for an interval of N₀ integers) with Q = 2^{k+1}Y gives
   203	  |U| ≤ (N₀ + Q²)/J, J = Σ_{d≤Q} μ²(d)∏_{ℓ|d} ω(ℓ)/(ℓ − ω(ℓ)).
   204	- Keeping only d = 1 and d = ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k: J ≥ |V| Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ.
   205	- By the PNT in the progression 3 mod 4 (effective for the fixed modulus 4),
   206	  Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ ≥ (1/2 − o(1))·ln 2/ln(2^{k+1}Y) ≥ c′/n.
   207	- |V| ≥ H²/2 for large H, since the coprime pairs number (6/π² + o(1))H². ∎
   208	
   209	**Lemma 10 (FULL is rare).** Fix q with d = ord_q(2) > T and a block k clean for q. Let
   210	F_k(q) be the set of integers p ∈ [P/2, P] such that some (v, ℓ) ∈ V × 𝒫_k has ℓ | Bp − Aq
   211	and d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ for some |δ| < m̄_k. Then
   212	
   213	  |F_k(q)| ≤ Σ_{v,ℓ,|δ|<m̄_k} (P·H/(2·2^kY·T) + 1)
   214	           ≤ 2|V||𝒫_k| m̄_k·(PH/(2^{k+1}YT) + 1).
   215	
   216	*Proof.*
   217	- Fix (v, ℓ, δ) and let g′ = gcd(A − B, d) ≤ H (A ≠ B, |A − B| < H).
   218	- The congruence (A−B)(p−1) ≡ ℓ²δ (mod d) is either unsolvable, or holds exactly on one
   219	  class of p mod d/g′.
   220	- ℓ ∤ d, because clean means ℓ ∤ q − 1. With p ≡ AqB^{−1} (mod ℓ), CRT gives one class mod
   221	  ℓd/g′ ≥ ℓd/H.
   222	- An interval of length P/2 contains ≤ P H/(2ℓd) + 1 such integers. ∎
   223	
   224	## 4. Proof of Theorem D′
   225	
   226	**(i) Correctness.** Only gcds with N in (1, N), or band primes dividing N, are output.
   227	
   228	**(ii) Cost (bit operations; M(b) = O(b log b) multiplication; per block, with T_k
   229	targets and m ≤ m̄_k).**
   230	
   231	| step | operations | bit cost |
   232	|---|---|---|
   233	| prime sieve to 16Y, V, Lo_v/Hi_v (isqrt of O(n)-bit integers) | O(Y log log Y) + O(H²) isqrt | Õ(Y + H²n) |
   234	| integer product tree of ℓ² (Σ bits = log₂L_k ≤ 3·2^kY) | balanced tree | O(M(Y)·log Y) |
   235	| exponent tree (per level Σ exponent bits ≤ log₂L_k + #nodes; ⌈log₂|𝒫_k|⌉ levels) + γ, γ^{−1} | O(Y log Y) mults mod N + 1 inversion | O(Y log Y·M(n)) |
   236	| roots (Euler test, powering mod ℓ), s, c, windows | O(\|V\|\|𝒫_k\| log Y) mults mod ℓ, ℓ² | Õ(H²Y) |
